  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The mass density function $\\lambda ,$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ of a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le 2$ is given by $\\lambda (x)=3{x}^{2}+1,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?",
  "worked_solution": "Given a thin rod oriented along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by  \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=3{x}^{2}+1,\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}+x]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =({2}^{3}+2)-({1}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $8\\,\\text{kg}.$",
